{"reference":"Matthew 14","book":"Matthew","chapter":14,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Herod and the Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Herod’s appearance at the chapter’s opening belongs within Matthew’s remembered first-century setting, shaped by Herodian client rule, Roman provincial authority, taxation, elite power, and crucifixion. This larger political frame may inform how readers understand the authority and vulnerability visible in the account of John’s imprisonment and death.","verse_refs":["Matthew 14:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_014_c93e7c14092a6562cb1d345f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}